## Findings

- **Near-miss on myself, mid-repro.** Driving the discovery-half sandbox
  repro I ran a bare (unescaped) `git init` + `commit --allow-empty`
  sequence and it landed on *my own worktree's branch* — ambient
  `GIT_DIR`/`GIT_WORK_TREE` caught me exactly as `prompts/strand.md` warns.
  Caught immediately (stray commit visible in `git log --oneline`),
  `reset --hard` back to the prior HEAD before anything was pushed or
  reported. No lasting damage. Redid the repro wrapped in
  `env -u GIT_DIR -u GIT_WORK_TREE`. Noted in the report as a live,
  on-topic case study for this exact ticket.
- **Environment trap, worked around, not fixed (out of scope).** Two
  existing tests in `test_daemon_child_git.py` spawn a rootless inner
  pytest subprocess whose `import brr` resolves the **host checkout's**
  installed copy (AGENTS.md's documented trap), not this worktree's WIP —
  so they can't see an uncommitted worktree-only change until it's
  installed somewhere on `sys.path`. Verified with a scratch venv
  (`/tmp/brr-hermeticity-venv`, `pip install -e ".[dev]"` against this
  worktree) and putting it first on `PATH` for both the outer `pytest -q`
  and the inner subprocess — everything green. `scripts/gate.py`'s own
  backend leg picks up the ambient `python` from `PATH`, so the same trick
  (`PATH="<scratch-venv>/bin:$PATH" python scripts/gate.py`) got a real
  GREEN gate run against this worktree's actual code, not the host's stale
  copy. Full detail + both gate runs (RED under the shared venv, GREEN
  under the scratch one) in the report.
